Documentation ensures that the project can be understood, used, and maintained by others. The main project report begins with the title page, certificate, acknowledgment, and abstract, followed by an introduction that explains the background, problem, objectives, and scope. It continues with a survey of existing systems, a system analysis that includes requirements and a feasibility study, and a design chapter with the architecture, diagrams, and database schema. The implementation chapter describes each module with screenshots, and the testing chapter presents the test plan, cases, and results. The report ends with a discussion of results, the conclusion and future scope, and the references and appendix. The feasibility study argues that the project is technically feasible because of the availability of language model APIs and web frameworks, economically feasible because development costs are low and mostly limited to API usage, and operationally feasible because the interface is simple enough for non-technical users. Supporting documents include a user manual, an installation and setup guide, API documentation, and a README file in the GitHub repository.
